From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Claude Agent <claude-agent@invalid>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 20:11:39 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] wayland/color-management: Accept mastering metadata and light
 levels
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

set_mastering_display_primaries and set_mastering_luminance raised
unsupported_feature, and set_max_cll and set_max_fall were ignored.
Accept all four, validate them as the protocol requires, store them as
the color state's target volume, and advertise
feature.set_mastering_display_primaries and
feature.extended_target_volume.

Protocol errors, as documented by wp_image_description_creator_params_v1:

 - Each of the four requests raises already_set when repeated.
 - set_mastering_luminance raises invalid_luminance unless the maximum
   is greater than the minimum, compared exactly in the protocol's
   units (max_lum * 10000 against min_lum).
 - create raises invalid_luminance when MaxFALL exceeds MaxCLL, and,
   for version 1 objects only, when MaxCLL or MaxFALL is not greater
   than the minimum or exceeds the maximum of the target luminance
   range (the mastering luminance if set, else the primary volume's).

A MaxCLL or MaxFALL of 0 means unknown in CTA-861-H, which the protocol
references. It counts as set but declares nothing and takes part in no
comparison, so content passing through an unknown level is not
disconnected.

Target volumes beyond the primary volume are supported as declared,
and extended_target_volume is advertised: target primaries are stored
and take part in identity, but no gamut is mapped with them, and the
target luminance only bounds the effective peak, so a target exceeding
the primary volume cannot cause artifacts. In particular:

 - Mastering display primaries go through the same checks as encoding
   primaries, and nothing more: coordinates within [-16, 16] and
   stored exactly (the conversion is now one helper for both
   requests), and a usable colorimetry by
   clutter_colorimetry_get_linear_bt2020_matrices(); anything else
   fails the image description with the 'unsupported' cause. They may
   lie outside the encoding primaries: DCI-P3, which most HDR10
   metadata declares, has its red primary just outside BT.2020.
 - The mastering luminance may exceed the primary volume's luminance
   range: HDR10 commonly declares a 0.0001 cd/m² minimum for PQ. The
   effective peak is bounded by both.
 - Reference white may exceed the primary volume's maximum luminance:
   set_luminances documents reaching it as requiring
   extended_target_volume. It raised invalid_luminance citing the
   missing feature, which no protocol version documents as an error;
   now that the feature is advertised it is accepted. The
   reference-white scaling of color transforms handles a reference
   above the maximum.

The values reach the color state through
clutter_color_state_params_new_with_target_volume(), in the units of
the encoding luminances (cd/m², as the protocol gives both). For an
image description created this way, the description record's target
luminance and primaries are now the declared target volume rather
than the encoding's, so the declaration takes part in record identity
as well as in color state equality.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01DLZiLBYev72J1s4D37L7Bi
